Subject: blink: never publish an empty root window rect to script

window.outerWidth and window.outerHeight report 0 on some launches while
innerWidth and innerHeight are populated. Measured on the shipped
macos-arm64 build of this series, six launches with identical argv,
--headless=new --window-size=1280,800, no DevTools client attached at any point,
reading the values at first script, at the load event, and 500ms later:

    run  at first script        at load        +500ms
      1  1280x800               1280x800       1280x800
      2  0x0, inner 1280x703    0x0            1280x800
      3  1280x800               1280x800       1280x800
      4  0x0, inner 1280x706    0x0            1280x800
      5  1280x800               1280x800       1280x800
      6  0x0, inner 1280x713    0x0            1280x800

Three of six, same command line every time, and --window-size does not prevent
it. So this is a race rather than a switch dependency, and the window it opens
is not small: the wrong value is still being served at the load event, which is
after every point a page is likely to read it.

It is worth separating this from the usual kind of finding. An outer window
smaller than its own viewport is not a weak signal to be weighed against
others, it is an impossibility, and zero is an impossibility twice over. A
detector needs no baseline for the machine, no corpus, no second visit and no
timing measurement. It needs `outerHeight < innerHeight || outerHeight === 0`,
evaluated in the document head, where fingerprinting script already runs. And
because it happens with no client attached, it applies to every user of this
browser whatever they drive it with, or whether they drive it at all.

The cause is two mojo calls that are not ordered with respect to each other.
WidgetBase::window_screen_rect_ is default-constructed empty and is written from
exactly one place, WidgetBase::UpdateScreenRects. The viewport size arrives
separately, on UpdateVisualProperties. Between the two the renderer holds a
populated viewport and a 0x0 window rect, and
ChromeClientImpl::RootWindowRect hands that rect to
LocalDOMWindow::outerWidth, outerHeight, screenX and screenY, which return its
fields directly. Upstream is aware the state exists - WebFrameWidgetImpl::
MoveWindowTo skips its pending-rect update when WindowRect() is empty and says
so in a comment - but nothing guarded the path that reaches JavaScript.

The substitute for an empty rect is the screen's available rect, read from the
same ScreenInfo the page is already being shown. That is the area a maximised
window occupies, and the page reads it back as screen.availWidth and
screen.availHeight, so the substituted window agrees with the screen surfaces
patches 0010 and 0041 compose instead of being a second and independently wrong
answer for the same window. It is also fixed at launch, which is the point: the
race is removed rather than narrowed, because the reported value stops depending
on whether an IPC has arrived.

WidgetBase::WindowRect() is deliberately left alone. Its emptiness is
load-bearing: MoveWindowTo and ResizeWindowTo branch on it, and making it
non-empty would feed a substituted rect into a real window-placement path. For
the same reason the four placement bindings - moveTo, moveBy, resizeTo, resizeBy
- keep reading the true rect, and only the four reporting bindings change.

Two things this does not fix, stated rather than implied.

The inner dimensions also move. Across the runs above innerHeight read 703, 706
and 713 before settling to 657, and a resize fires shortly after load. That is a
different mechanism - the visual viewport being sized from provisional visual
properties and then corrected - and this patch does not address it. It is a
weaker signal than the impossible one, because a viewport that changes size
early is something a real browser does when a window is being restored, but it
is a real residual and it belongs to whoever owns the headless display rows.

A page that reads outer dimensions before the browser's real rect arrives and
again afterwards can still see the value change, from the available rect to the
true window rect. Closing that requires the browser process to deliver the
window rect at widget creation rather than on a later call, which is a change in
a different process and a different patch. What is closed here is that the value
a page is given is always a possible one.

Also not papered over: a CDP client that sends
Emulation.setDeviceMetricsOverride can still make innerHeight exceed
outerHeight, because the override changes the viewport and not the window.
Puppeteer's default 800x600 viewport does exactly that. That is a driver default
rather than a browser defect, the wrapper packages set defaultViewport to null
to avoid it, and lying about the window to match an emulated viewport would be
the wrong repair.

Compile-unverified: this cannot be built in this session, and the after-state is
not measured. The before-state above is measured.

Ledger: headless.synthetic-display, screen.outer-dimensions.

--- a/third_party/blink/renderer/core/frame/local_dom_window.cc
